    "field": "Climate change",
    "problem": "Climate simulations struggle to accurately model historical ocean warming and sea-level rise because they often fail to incorporate the substantial, multi-decadal cooling effect of major volcanic eruptions, which offsets a large fraction of anthropogenic warming.",
    "method": "To isolate the long-term oceanic impact of volcanic eruptions, the authors compared two ensembles of climate model simulations for 1880-2000: one including time-varying volcanic forcings like the 1883 Krakatoa eruption and one without.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze a suite of climate models to demonstrate that the 1883 Krakatoa eruption substantially reduced ocean warming and sea-level rise well into the twentieth century, offsetting a large fraction of anthropogenic effects.",
